diagnosis_text:
The internal web portal (HTTP service) on web_server_1 is healthy and reachable. From the affected client host_1_1_1_1, DNS resolution for web1.local returns 10.200.0.4 (getent hosts), ICMP to 10.200.0.4 succeeds with low latency, and HTTP fetches complete successfully with HTTP/1.1 200 OK in ~3–5 ms total (curl timings). A second client (host_2_1_1_1) also retrieves web1.local with HTTP 200 and similar response times, ruling out a path-specific problem.

On the server itself (web_server_1), the HTTP stack is up: port 80 is listening on 0.0.0.0 with apache2 (ss/netstat shows LISTEN on :80 by apache2 PID 57), multiple apache2 worker processes are running, and an HTTP request to localhost returns “HTTP/1.1 200 OK.” This confirms the web service is functioning on the target host and interface (web_server_1 eth0 on the server VLAN via switch_server_access).

The observed user symptom (“page has not loaded all morning”) is not reproducible and does not correlate with any current network or server failure. Root cause: no active fault found; service is operational end-to-end at this time.

Corrective fix: No restart is required. Advise the user to retry (clearing browser/DNS cache if needed) and monitor. If intermittent issues recur, capture timestamps and client details so we can correlate with logs and network telemetry, but currently the portal and underlying network/routing appear normal. 
